# Review before PR

Run this sequence in the same task when the user asks. Finish after the merge. Push a tag only when the PR changes product behavior. Do not commit or tag while a gate is red.

1. Review the current diff against this repo's rules (`.cursor/rules/`), structure, patterns, and style. Stack: Client (Vite/React/antd) + Server (Koa/Mongoose) + MongoDB + Redis. Prefer MIT/Apache deps; never reintroduce `vm2`.
2. Confirm the diff implements the requested feature or bugfix, does not add a new problem, and the affected behavior works. Gates: `make check` (format-check + lint + unit tests). Prefer `make test` / `make lint` over ad-hoc scripts. Apply PATH from `~/.zshrc` before shell commands. Reuse a green gate already run in this task; re-run only if a later edit invalidates it.
3. Leave out unrelated or non-compliant edits. Do not commit them just because they are dirty.
4. Several features or bugfixes may share one branch and one PR. Commit each feature or bugfix on its own (Conventional Commits; Husky pre-commit + commit-msg). Do not put two features, or a feature and an unrelated fix, in the same commit. Do not use `git add -i` or `git rebase -i`. Escape hatch `SKIP_TESTS=1` / `--no-verify` only sparingly and never to hide a red gate.
5. On latest `main`, pull and create that dev branch. When the PR changes product behavior, bump the shipped version once before the last commit. Keep these in lockstep: root `package.json`, `Client/package.json`, `Server/package.json`, `Helm/apiadmin/Chart.yaml` (`version`, `appVersion`), and the app image `tag` in `Helm/apiadmin/values.yaml`. Pass the same semver as `APP_VERSION` when building images. A single feature may include that bump in its only commit. Skip the version bump when the PR has no functional change (rules, formatting, comments, docs-only). Do not bump `Plugins/*/package.json` for an app release.
6. Push the branch once, open one PR with `gh pr create`, then merge with `gh pr merge --merge` so each commit stays on `main`. Do not squash.
7. Check out `main` and pull so the local branch matches the merged result.
8. Tag that `main` commit `vX.Y.Z`, then push the tag once with `git push origin vX.Y.Z`, only when the PR changes product behavior. A `v*` or `release-*` tag on `main` publishes images via `.github/workflows/publish-images.yml` (GHCR `ghcr.io/hanyouqing/apiadmin` and Docker Hub `hanyouqing/apiadmin`) and the Helm chart to `gh-pages`. Push to `main` alone also pushes an immutable tag and `latest`. Do not tag a PR that only changes rules, formatting, comments, or other non-functional files. A dependency audit fix still gets a tag.
9. After the sequence finishes, or stops because a step failed, start the speech loop (below). Success says `apiadmin vibing finished`. Failure says `apiadmin vibing Failed`.

```bash
git fetch origin
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b feat/short-topic
git add <feature-or-fix-paths>
git commit
git add <next-feature-or-fix-paths>
git commit
# bump the version files above when product behavior changes, then
git add <version-paths>
git commit
git push -u origin HEAD
gh pr create
gh pr merge --merge
git checkout main
git pull origin main
# skip the tag when the PR has no functional change
git tag vX.Y.Z
git push origin vX.Y.Z
```

Do not force-push `main`. Do not skip hooks. Do not commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, `*.tfstate`, Docker Hub tokens). Do not tag the feature branch. Do not push the tag before the merge. Update `.cursor/rules/` with durable session lessons before the commit that ships them (see `commit-push-session-rules.mdc`).

## Voice

When this sequence ends, start one background speech loop and a macOS dialog with a single **Stop** button. Speech repeats until the user clicks **Stop** or closes the dialog. Do not wait on the dialog. Tell the user the Stop button is on screen.

```bash
python3 - "apiadmin vibing finished" "apiadmin" <<'PY'
import os, signal, subprocess, sys
phrase, title = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
loop = subprocess.Popen(
    ["bash", "-c", 'while true; do say "$1"; done', "bash", phrase],
    start_new_session=True,
)
script = f'display dialog "{phrase}" buttons {{"Stop"}} default button "Stop" with title "{title}"'
try:
    subprocess.run(["osascript", "-e", script], check=False)
finally:
    os.killpg(loop.pid, signal.SIGTERM)
PY
```

Use `"apiadmin vibing Failed"` as the phrase when a gate stays red or the sequence stops before the merge.

## Dependency audit

If the production dependency audit (CI step, `make audit`, or `npm audit --omit=dev --audit-level=high` on root / Client / Server) reports a high or critical advisory, fix it in the same task before finishing:

1. Fix it in the release change. Prefer a direct dependency bump or an existing npm `override`. Do not leave the audit red. Prefer maintained replacements (`@faker-js/faker`, `@koa/multer`, `@node-saml/node-saml`).
2. Push that fix. If the PR is still open, push the branch. If it is already merged, put the fix on a branch from latest `main`, open a PR, merge it, check out `main`, and pull.
3. If a `vX.Y.Z` tag for this release was already pushed, delete that remote tag (`git push origin :refs/tags/vX.Y.Z`) and the local tag (`git tag -d vX.Y.Z`). Do not delete any other tag. Do not force-push `main`.
4. Tag the new `main` commit that contains the fix, then push the tag once with `git push origin vX.Y.Z`. Reuse the same tag name only when that version is not already published on the registries; otherwise bump the patch and tag the new version.

```bash
git push origin :refs/tags/vX.Y.Z
git tag -d vX.Y.Z
git tag vX.Y.Z
git push origin vX.Y.Z
```

## Review cost

On every review, spend tokens only on what this change still needs:

- Read and diff only the files the change touches. Do not re-scan the whole repo or re-read files already understood.
- Reuse results already produced in the task (`make check`, browser checks, docker context checks). Do not repeat a passing quality gate or a long command unless a later edit invalidates it.
- Keep the review note short: state the decision and the few findings that change what gets committed. Do not paste large diffs or logs.
- Leave unrelated dirty files out, so they are not re-reviewed or committed.
- Do not launch extra agents or broad searches for a review that the current diff already answers.

## Docker build context

When a change touches an image build (`Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore`, `Scripts/build-docker.sh`, compose, publish workflow), check `.dockerignore` in the same review:

- Exclude editor/agent dirs (`.cursor`, `.git`), CI (`.github`), docs (`Docs`, most `*.md`), compose files, tests, caches, local env files, `Helm/`, and `node_modules`. These do not belong in the build context.
- Keep every file the `Dockerfile` copies and the build actually runs: root `package*.json`, `Client/`, `Server/`, `Scripts/`, `Utils/`, `Plugins/`, `tsconfig*.json`, `vite.config.ts`, and the `Dockerfile` itself. Keep `README.md` / `README_zh.md` if still allowlisted.
- Scan the context for secrets: `.env*`, private keys, certificates, credential files, Terragrunt state. Public client ids are not secrets. Pass image-only values with build args (`APP_VERSION`, `BUILD_*`); do not copy untracked env files.
- The runtime stage copies build output only (`Client/dist` → `Static`, `Server/dist`, production `Server` deps, `Plugins`). Source, docs, and Helm stay in the builder stage or out of the context.
